WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.089
Stephen King's books have been adapted to
film or television over 100 times, making

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:08.750
him the all-time "King" of adaptations from
a living author's work.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.060
But while some adaptations of his works rank
among the most beloved films of all time,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.370
others are hardly worth remembering at all.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.860
To help you parse out the good from the bad,
here's a list of the 10 worst and 10 best

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.929
Stephen King adaptations so far, not in any
particular order, but starting with the ones

00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:27.759
that probably don't deserve your time.

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:28.759
Cell

00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:33.330
Nine years after appearing together in 1408
— which we'll be talking about much later

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.290
— John Cusack and Samuel L. Jackson reunited
for Cell.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.070
Unfortunately, it wasn't a reunion really
worth having.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.481
The plot centers on Clay Riddell, a man who's
determined to reunite with his son after a

00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:49.199
malevolent electronic signal turns cell phone
users into mindless killers.

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:53.480
Along the way, he teams up with train driver
Tom McCourt in order to better their chances

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.480
of survival.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.940
While the novel was generally well-received
for it's technophobic take on the zombie trope,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.899
the movie suffered a far less positive fate.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:03.640
It stunk.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.640
Graveyard Shift

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.850
1990's Graveyard Shift still stands as one
of the worst King adaptations.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.200
In a Maine textile mill where a drifter has
taken a job, several overnight employees have

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.409
died, and the egomaniacal foreman sends the
drifter — and others — down into the basement

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:20.579
to investigate.

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:23.509
King's short story didn't translate well to
the screen.

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:27.270
The saving grace of the film is a crazy exterminator
played by Brad…

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.870
"This place is infested."

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.720
"That might be the understatement of the year."

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.110
...but even that's not enough to save this
movie.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:36.140
Thinner

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:41.550
Despite having excellent source material,
Tom Holland's 1990 adaptation of Thinner just

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.030
couldn't pull it off.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.369
When an obese and morally bankrupt lawyer
Billy Halleck accidentally runs over and kills

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:51.250
a gypsy woman, he uses his court connections
to get off the hook.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:56.050
The woman's elderly father takes revenge by
placing a curse upon Halleck, one which soon

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.820
has him uncontrollably losing weight to the
point of emaciation.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.500
The premise is definitely scary, but the effect
falls flat on the big screen — especially

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.789
when we're confronted with the actor in a
terribly fake latex fat suit that only gets

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.720
slightly less hilarious as he sheds the pounds.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.940
Maximum Overdrive

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.880
Stephen King himself stepped behind the lens
for the 1986 adaptation of his own Maximum

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:19.880
Overdrive.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.840
"It was my first picture as a director, and
you know something?

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.069
I sort of enjoyed it!"

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:26.430
Others, however, did not.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.790
The movie puts Earth in the tail of a comet,
after which once-inert machines suddenly come

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.080
to life and go on a murderous rampage against
humans.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.140
"Look out!"

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:41.960
[Screaming]

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.680
At a truck stop, a bland protagonist played
by Emilio Estevez and an odd assortment of

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.680
other unremarkable characters settle in to
wait out the living trucks that have them

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.130
pinned down inside.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.849
While Maximum Overdrive does have a killer
soundtrack from AC/DC and a few neat shots

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:01.920
peppered throughout, the movie is still a
maximum dud.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
Needful Things

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.520
Although the novel Needful Things is a favorite
among King fans, the 1993 film adaptation

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.849
of the story might leave audiences ready to
sell their own souls to the Devil just to

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:14.110
get it over with.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:18.950
Set in Castle Rock, Maine, Needful Things
follows a character named Leland Gaunt opening

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.590
an antiques store called — you guessed it
— Needful Things.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.670
Many of the town's residents soon find their
hearts' desires fulfilled by the store's items

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.820
— some of which have a distinctly supernatural
bent.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.390
But Gaunt's prices are nearly too good to
be true.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.260
Rather than heaps of cash, Gaunt just wants
the townspeople to play pranks on eachother

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.220
… but those small misdeeds end up igniting
some intense rivalries, and things turn violent

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.220
very quickly.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.980
"We're having fun now."

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.439
If that sounds like an intriguing story, that's
because it is.

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:51.239
King's written version of Needful Things is
certainly worthy of your time.

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:55.720
But the movie's failure to translate the character
development of the many players involved renders

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.200
this movie a total wash.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.931
At least we got a pretty great Rick and Morty
episode out of it:

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.640
"A typewriter that generates best-selling
murder mysteries, and then makes the murders

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.840
happen in real life?

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.160
Woooooo!"

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.460
"Be quiet."

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:10.680
The Mangler

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:16.610
Tobe Hooper's 1994 adaptation of King's short
story The Mangler somehow spawned two sequels,

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.760
but the original was not actually very good.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.070
The movie stars Freddie Kreuger actor Robert
Englund as sinister laundry-service owner

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.850
whose machine has been possessed by a demon
that's been activated by the presence of blood...and

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.010
over-the-counter antacids.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.010
Yes, really.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:35.690
The original short story was pretty threadbare
to begin with, but the movie completely ignored

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:39.220
the opportunity to make a campy pic that pokes
fun at itself.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.450
Instead, we're left with a ridiculous and
gory flick that goes on way too long and takes

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.540
itself much too seriously.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.890
Riding the Bullet

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:52.610
When King published his 2000 novella Riding
the Bullet, it was generally well-received

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.450
by critics and made history as the first mass-distributed
e-book.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:00.980
The story follows Alan Parker, a neurotic
and depressed college student whose obsession

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:04.390
with worst-case scenarios nearly drives him
to suicide.

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:08.260
After receiving news that his mother has had
a stroke, Parker decides to hitchhike so he

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.190
can reach her before she passes.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.580
But instead of culminating in an exciting
thrill ride, the movie renders the journey

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.920
completely boring.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.660
Add this to your skip list.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.170
Dreamcatcher

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:25.100
Even Stephen King didn't completely love his
2001 novel Dreamcatcher, but the resulting

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.480
movie was even worse.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.740
The film follows four lifelong friends — who
have psychic powers, of course — on a winter

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.600
hunting trip to upstate Maine.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.120
They soon find themselves caught between invading
aliens and the U.S. military forces trying

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.460
to contain them.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:44.139
Thomas Jane, Damian Lewis, Timothy Olyphant,
and Jason Lee do fine work as the friends,

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:49.400
but Morgan Freeman falls flat as an overzealous
commander with ridiculously distracting eyebrows.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.750
What results is an overly long, free-associating
mess of a movie that could have been much

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:57.240
better if it'd just stuck to one tone.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.000
The Lawnmower Man

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.880
If you're a fan of this short story from King's
1978 collection Night Shift, don't be fooled

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:09.160
by Brett Leonard's 1992 film The Lawnmower
Man — it's an adaptation in name only.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.700
Other than the title and a single scene, the
movie bears little resemblance to the source

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:14.700
material.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.490
Instead of a nutty little tale about a lawn
mowing service gone horribly wrong, the film

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:24.090
steals from the short story Flowers for Algernon
and Mary Shelly's Frankenstein for its mish-mashed

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.090
plot.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.160
The movie centers on a doctor who chooses
a mentally impaired greenskeeper to be the

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.720
subject of his experiments in intelligence-boosting
via virtual reality and a drug cocktail.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.350
Follow King's lead on this one, and give the
lousy story of The Lawnmower Man a hard pass.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:41.880
The Night Flier

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.160
The Night Flier stays fairly true to King's
original short story, but that doesn't mean

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.930
that this 1997 adaptation is actually a good
movie.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:54.920
Miguel Ferrer plays tabloid reporter Richard
Dees, who attempts to hunt down a serial killer

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.660
who uses a small aircraft to travel from town
to town — leaving a grisly trail of victims

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.720
in his wake.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.610
Dees soon finds that this is no ordinary criminal,
and must also deal with a competing reporter

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.990
who's trying her hardest to steal his scoop.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:11.850
While the short story's version of Dees was
an unlikeable cynic who finds his worldview

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:16.780
shaken, the movie's script and Ferrer's flat
performance makes Dees a complete jerk with

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.580
no redeeming qualities.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.610
If you decide to watch The Night Flier, you'll
probably find yourself rooting for the bad

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:24.560
guys to win before it's all said and done.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.370
Now that we've gotten through the shlock,
here are the best Stephen King adaptations

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:29.990
so far.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:31.080
Carrie

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.980
For over 40 years, Brian De Palma's 1976 adaptation
of Carrie has remained a classic.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.810
Released just two years after the book's publication,
Carrie was the first adaptation of a Stephen

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.480
King work, and it's still arguably the best.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.260
Featuring Sissy Spacek in the title role,
this supernatural tale sets the hook of suspense

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.390
and reels in the viewer without remorse.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:57.010
Spacek is at turns ethereal and terrifying
as Carrie, and Piper Laurie delivers a commanding

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.790
performance as her oppressive mother Margaret.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.680
Even though it's a little dated today, the
original vision of Carrie still holds up as

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.840
a must-watch Stephen King movie.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.770
Stand By Me

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:13.410
Unlike many King adaptations, Stand By Me
is more coming-of-age drama than horror.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:18.000
Based on King's semi-autobiographical short
story The Body, it follows four adolescent

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.130
boys in a small Oregon town who embark on
a journey to find the corpse of a missing

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:23.130
boy.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.910
Thanks to the off-screen friendship forged
by actors Wil Wheaton, River Phoenix, Jerry

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:31.720
O'Connell, and Corey Feldman, the authenticity
of the characters' connection was incredibly

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:32.720
real.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.240
Stand By Me remains a classic today, and even
those who don't like horror films can enjoy

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.400
this Stephen King story.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.909
The Dead Zone

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:45.460
David Cronenberg's 1983 adaptation of Stephen
King's The Dead Zone remains a more accessible

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:47.690
movie than King's more horrific works.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:52.690
The story focuses on Johnny Smith, a man who
wakes up from a coma to discover he has unexplained

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:58.160
psychic powers, and finds himself on a collision
course with destiny during a pivotal election.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.950
It's a masterpiece of thrilling and suspenseful
storytelling that strings viewers along before

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.620
the final climactic scene.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:07.570
The Shawshank Redemption

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:12.500
Although it registered very little impact
in theaters upon its release, director Frank

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:16.990
Darabont's 1994 prison drama The Shawshank
Redemption has gone on to become one of the

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.740
best-loved films of the last 30 years.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.990
The movie tells the story of two prisoners
serving life sentences at the Shawshank State

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:28.500
Penitentiary, who become dear friends despite
their difficult circumstances.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.080
Starring Tim Robbins and Morgan Freeman in
the lead roles, The Shawshank Redemption is

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.620
a gritty tale with a lot of heart and plot
points that transcend the generations.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:38.620
The Shining

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:43.560
"All work and no play" might make Jack a dull
boy, but The Shining most certainly isn't

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.560
dull.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:48.620
Stanley Kubrick's 1980 version of The Shining
still stands as one of the most beloved King

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.240
adaptations, even if the author didn't care
for it himself.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.900
The movie follows writer Jack Torrance, his
wife Wendy, and their young son Danny as they

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.870
travel to Colorado to become the winter caretakers
of the opulent — and haunted — Overlook

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:03.000
Hotel.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.870
Danny's psychic abilities allow him to see
the terrifying past of the Overlook, and his

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:11.490
troubled father's inner demons are exploited
by the spirits still haunting the hotel.

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:16.760
The tale deviates significantly from the source
material, in both characterization and storyline.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:21.610
Nevertheless, The Shining continues to be
a favorite among horror lovers.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.070
Misery

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:29.050
A psychological thriller directed by Rob Reiner
about fan obsession gone horribly wrong, 1990's

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:33.590
Misery was a critical and commercial success
that launched Kathy Bates' career — and

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:34.770
for good reason.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:39.250
The plot follows romance author Paul Sheldon
as he wakes up from a car accident to find

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.200
himself in the care of a superfan named Annie
Wilkes.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:46.090
"You and I were meant to be together forever."

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.220
When Wilkes learns that Sheldon has killed
off her favorite character in his latest novel,

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.220
things start to get grim, and there are some
scenes of torment that won't soon be forgotten

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:57.100
or forgiven by fans of this film.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:58.690
The Green Mile

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:03.950
Director Frank Darabont returned to the King
library for another prison drama with 1999's

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:07.240
The Green Mile, starring Tom Hanks and Michael
Clarke Duncan.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:11.870
This flashback film is told from the perspective
of Paul Edgecomb, who recounts supernatural

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:16.590
events he experienced as a corrections officer
after a very special prisoner arrived on death

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:18.790
row at the Cold Mountain Penitentiary.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:23.140
It's a soulful and thoughtful movie which
will leave most viewers profoundly affected

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.420
after the credits roll.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.200
Dolores Claiborne

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.140
Kathy Bates returned to the world of Stephen
King when she appeared in the 1995 psychological

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.480
thriller Dolores Claiborne, based on the novel
of the same name and directed by Taylor Hackford.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:42.270
The film follows the tale of a domestic servant
who's accused of murdering her elderly employer.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:47.050
It's a harrowing and suspenseful tale that
will keep audiences guessing until the end.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:48.050
1408

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:55.100
In the 2007 psychological horror film 1408,
John Cusack does what he does best: he plays

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.310
a cynic with love problems.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:01.600
Starring as Mike Enslin, a skeptical author
who decides to check into a notoriously haunted

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.440
hotel room in order to investigate it, he's
joined by Samuel L. Jackson as hotel manager

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:07.900
Gerald Olin.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.810
What follows is a tense and terrifying descent
into Enslin's psyche.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:15.940
Although it's definitely not on the level
of The Shining, 1408 is still a satisfying

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:20.820
and scary haunted hotel effort that manages
to generate genuine thrills without relying

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.400
on bucketfuls of gore.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.400
It

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.070
The feature film debut of It smashed all sorts
of records on its opening weekend and for

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:33.750
good reason: it's a very decent adaptation
of one of King's most prized books.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:39.180
The 1990 TV mini-series successfully translated
the scares of the novel as well, but its budgeted

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:42.980
special effects and bloated acting moments
have kept that version from aging well in

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:44.210
the years since.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:48.940
The new version of It, however, is much sharper
and more intense, and also presents the heart

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.310
and wit of the novel's characters.

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:55.251
Scary clowns might be a cultural phenomenon
that makes Pennywise a timely terror, but

00:12:55.251 --> 00:12:58.230
count on this one to be more than just a flash-in-the-pan
favorite.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.300
"You'll float too.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:01.520
You'll float too.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.600
You'll float too, you'll float too, you'll
float too!"

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.680
Thanks for watching!

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.590
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:10.000
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

